<setup>

  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="1" to="1" step="1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="k" from="0.1" to="0.4" step="0.1"/>
  
  <function name="f">ln(x)</function>
  <function name="fP">1/x</function>
  <function name="fPP">-1/x^2</function>
  <function name="fPPP">2/x^3</function>
  <function name="fP4">-6/x^4</function>
  
  <math name="c0">$$f($a)</math>
  <math name="c1">$$fP($a)</math>
  <math name="c2">$$fPP($a)/2</math>
  <math name="c3">$$fPPP($a)/3!</math>
  <math name="c4">$$fP4($a)/4!</math>

  <math simplify name="c">$a + $k</math>
  
  <function name="T4">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a) + $c2*(x-$a)^2 + $c3*(x-$a)^3 + $c4*(x-$a)^4</function>
  <function name="T4Symb">c_0 + c_1*(x-$a) + c_2*(x-$a)^2 + c_3*(x-$a)^3 + c_4*(x-$a)^4</function>
  
  <function name="T3">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a) + $c2*(x-$a)^2 + $c3*(x-$a)^3</function>
  <function name="T3Symb">c_0 + c_1*(x-$a) + c_2*(x-$a)^2 + c_3*(x-$a)^3</function>

  <function name="T2">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a) + $c2*(x-$a)^2</function>
  <function name="T2Symb">c_0 + c_1*(x-$a) + c_2*(x-$a)^2</function>

  <function name="T1">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a)</function>
  <function name="T1Symb">c_0 + c_1*(x-$a)</function>
  
</setup>

<p>
  Consider the function <m>f(x) = \ln(x)</m>, suppose we want to use Taylor polynomials to approximate the value of <m>\ln(x)</m> near <m>a = $a</m>.
</p>

<!--
<p>
  <m>pm = $pm</m>, <m>k = $k</m>
</p>
-->

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
         Find and state the degree <m>3</m> Taylor polynomial of <m>f</m>  centered at <m>a = $a</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>T_3(x) = </m><answer name="T3Entered" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$T3</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Use <m>T_3(x)</m> to estimate the value of <m>f($c) = \ln($c)</m>.  Report your result to an accuracy of at least <m>0.000001</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\ln($c) = f($c) \approx T_3($c) = </m><answer name="fEst" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$$T3($c)</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Finally, we use the Lagrange Error Bound to quantify the error in our approximation in part (b).  Fill in the blanks below appropriately; report any decimal values to an accuracy of at least <m>0.000001</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Note that the fourth derivative of <m>f(x)</m> is <m>f^{(4)}(x) = -\frac{6}{x^4}</m>, and on the interval <m>[$a, $c]</m>, the absolute maximum value of <m>|f^{(4)}(x)| = |\frac{6}{x^4}|</m> is      
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>M = </m><answer>abs($$fP4($a))</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        In addition, we identify the values of other key components of the Lagrange Error Bound formula, <m>(c-a)</m> and <m>(n+1)</m>:
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>c-a = </m><answer>$k</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>n + 1 = </m><answer>4</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        Using these values of <m>M</m>, <m>|c - a|</m>, and <m>(n+1)</m>,  in the Lagrange Error Bound formula, it follows that  
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>|f($c) - T_3($c)| \le </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>abs($$fP4($a))*$k^4/4!</answer>
      </p>
      
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>